<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Makhanda (prophet)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nxele, Makana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makhanda">Makhanda</a>, also called Nxele, was a Xhosa prophet and war leader. In the Fifth Frontier War he led an assault by some 10,000 Xhosa on the British garrison at Grahamstown on 22 April 1819, which was repulsed. He surrendered and was imprisoned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robben-island">Robben Island</a>, and drowned in 1820 while escaping. The city of Grahamstown was renamed Makhanda in his honour in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/makhanda-prophet.jpg" alt="Makhanda (prophet)" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thembani Onceya · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Makhanda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Makhanda was born near the coast around 1780 in the Uitenhage area. His father, Gwala, was a Xhosa of the Cwerha clan, and his mother a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> of the Gqunukhwebe clan. After his father died when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makhanda">Makhanda</a> was a boy, he was raised by his mother, a spiritual diviner and medicine woman, and was later recognised as an inyanga. It is unclear when he first met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>; the missionary Dr James van der Kemp had founded a mission at Bethelsdorp in 1799, and Makhanda may have known him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As an itinerant preacher he advocated peace and denounced the use of magic. Around 1812 Chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndlambe">Ndlambe</a> appointed him an advisor and military doctor, and he discussed theology with the chaplain at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makhanda">Grahamstown</a> and the missionary John Read, who described him as a stout and handsome man who commanded respect. In 1816 his view of the missionaries changed. He had seen the mistreatment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> farmers, and the Xhosa had suffered the expulsion of 20,000 people from the Zuurveld in 1812. He came to see the conflict as a contest between Thixo, the god of the whites, and Mdalidiphu, the god of the Xhosa, and preached a fusion of the two that leaned toward Xhosa belief.</p><p>In 1818 he fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-amalinde">Battle of Amalinde</a> against Chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngqika">Ngqika</a>, an ally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. After a force under Colonel Thomas Brereton seized 23,000 head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndlambe">Ndlambe</a>'s people, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makhanda">Makhanda</a> urged the Xhosa to unite and drive out the British, promising that their bullets would turn to water. The attack of 22 April 1819, under the overall command of Ndlambe's son Mdushane, was made in daylight, with women and children following to occupy the town; about 350 British troops and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> group led by Jan Boesak repulsed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was held on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robben-island">Robben Island</a> with private quarters, food and furniture. On 25 December 1820 he tried to escape with thirty other prisoners; only four survived the crossing. Because he had promised never to abandon his people, they waited about fifty years before holding his funeral rites. He is regarded as one of the first Africans to attempt a synthesis of African and European beliefs, and political prisoners on Robben Island, Robert Mangaliso Sobukwe among them, later petitioned for the island to be renamed after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Makhanda (prophet)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Fourth Frontier War of 1811–1812 British troops drove the Xhosa east of the Fish River and founded Grahamstown, which in 1819 survived an attack led by the Xhosa prophet <b>Makhanda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Uitenhage, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.75,25.4&amp;year=1810" aria-label="Uitenhage on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Makhanda (prophet)"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4673 5327h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4673 5327h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.75,25.4&amp;year=1810">What was here in 1810? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
